---
// A door, not a second copy of /research/. One screen: what the work is, three pieces
// of evidence a reader can open, and where to go next. Sent directly to people coming
// from the biology side.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import site from '../data/site.json';

const points = [
  {
    head: 'State the outcome, not the protocol',
    body: 'An archive of experiments is indexed by what was done: a compound, a gene, a stimulation pattern. It is not indexed by what the cells ended up doing. I learn the inverse map, so a goal written in ordinary English returns an intervention and its dose, and a refusal when the archive contains nothing that matches.',
  },
  {
    head: 'Supervision where there are no labels',
    body: 'Nobody annotated those experiments with the question you are now asking, so the supervision has to be manufactured: predicates defined over measurements made for other reasons, or a vision-language model used as a second measuring instrument and validated, behaviour by behaviour, against the physical one.',
  },
  {
    head: 'Evaluation designed as carefully as the model',
    body: 'Every result is reported beside a baseline that never reads the request. On a public drug-screening archive that baseline scored 0.833 where my model scored 0.842, which is how I found that per-cell image features are entangled with cell death. A lower number that means something is worth more than a higher number that does not.',
  },
];

const evidence = [
  {
    kind: 'Human cells, imaging',
    title: 'Most of It Was Toxicity',
    body: 'Goal-conditioned retrieval of compounds and doses from a public Cell Painting archive, with dose windows and calibrated refusal, and the measurement artefact that had been inflating phenotype models on it.',
    links: [
      { label: 'Preprint', href: '/papers/paint-by-words-toxicity.pdf' },
      { label: 'Write-up', href: '/blog/most-of-it-was-toxicity/' },
      { label: 'Code', href: 'https://github.com/drnamlabs-gif/paint-by-words' },
    ],
  },
  {
    kind: 'Biobots and organoids',
    title: 'Toward Controlling Biology with Language',
    body: 'Offline learning of prompt-conditioned interventions from an archive of past stimulation experiments, with a vision-language model judging outcomes from video as the only reward. Work with Tufts University; a wet lab on the other end of the loop.',
    links: [{ label: 'arXiv:2610.02247', href: 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02247' }],
  },
  {
    kind: 'Simulated cells',
    title: 'Free-form language prompting for cellular control',
    body: 'The first instantiation of the loop, where a sentence becomes a spatial field acting on a population of cells, and generalises to instructions never trained on.',
    links: [{ label: 'arXiv:2509.10660', href: 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10660' }],
  },
  {
    kind: 'Language models from scratch',
    title: 'CellBio-GPT',
    body: 'A 40.5M-parameter model written from scratch in PyTorch and trained against a tissue simulator rather than against human preference: tokenizer, pretraining, fine-tuning, DPO, then reinforcement learning with the simulator as the reward.',
    links: [
      { label: 'Demo', href: '/cellbio/' },
      { label: 'Code', href: 'https://github.com/drnamlabs-gif/cellbio-gpt' },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Base
  current="AI for biology"
  title="AI for biology"
  description="Using language models to state what a biological system should do, and learning the intervention that does it from archives of experiments already run. Cells, organoids, biobots, and a public drug-screening archive."
>
  <section class="head">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">One of two lines of work</p>
      <h1>AI for biology</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        I use language models to say what a biological system should do, and learn the
        intervention that does it from experiments that have already been run. The same
        loop has been built four times now, on deliberately unlike systems: simulated
        cells, living biobots and organoids, human cells under a microscope, and a
        transcriptomic archive.
      </p>
      <p class="small muted">
        The biobot and organoid work is postdoctoral research at the University of Vermont
        in Josh Bongard's laboratory, with Michael Levin's group at Tufts, supported by the
        U.S. Army Research Office. The Cell Painting work below is independent and received
        no specific funding.
      </p>

      <figure class="shot">
        <video autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata"
          aria-label="A request is typed, a ranking of compounds and doses appears with a toxicity score beside the model's own, then the real microscopy wells behind two of those rows.">
          <source src="/blog/paint-by-words/00_demo.webm" type="video/webm" />
          <source src="/blog/paint-by-words/00_demo.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
        </video>
        <figcaption>
          Ask for a phenotype in plain English and the archive answers with a compound,
          a dose and the evidence. Four of the five rows score higher on <i>kill most of
          the cells</i> than on what was asked; the real wells behind two of them are
          shown next. <a href="/blog/most-of-it-was-toxicity/">The write-up</a>.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <figure class="shot">
        <video autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata"
          aria-label="One compound photographed at eight increasing doses, nuclei and actin channels, on a single brightness scale; the well empties as the dose rises.">
          <source src="/blog/paint-by-words/02_dose_series.webm" type="video/webm" />
          <source src="/blog/paint-by-words/02_dose_series.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
        </video>
        <figcaption>
          Nothing modelled here: one compound at its eight doses, on a single brightness
          scale taken from the lowest, so the thinning out is real. This is why the
          output has to be able to rise and then fall.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">What the work is</p>
      <div class="cards">
        {points.map((p) => (
          <article class="card">
            <h2 class="card__head">{p.head}</h2>
            <p class="card__body">{p.body}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Evidence you can open</p>
      <div class="ev">
        {evidence.map((e) => (
          <article class="ev__item">
            <p class="ev__kind mono">{e.kind}</p>
            <h2 class="ev__title">{e.title}</h2>
            <p class="ev__body">{e.body}</p>
            <p class="ev__links">
              {e.links.map((l) => (<a href={l.href}>{l.label}</a>))}
            </p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Next</p>
      <p class="lede">
        The research pages carry the full account, including the strands this page leaves
        out. The other line of work is markets.
      </p>
      <div class="hero__actions">
        <a class="btn btn--primary" href="/research/">Research in full</a>
        <a class="btn" href="/publications/">Publications</a>
        <a class="btn" href="/ai-for-finance/">AI for markets</a>
        <a class="btn" href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>Contact</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .cards { display: grid; gap: 1rem; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(16rem, 1fr)); }
  .card {
    border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 10px;
    background: var(--bg-raised); padding: 1.1rem 1.2rem;
  }
  .card__head { font-size: 1.02rem; margin: 0 0 .45rem; }
  .card__body { margin: 0; color: var(--text-soft); font-size: .95rem; line-height: 1.6; }

  .ev { display: grid; gap: 1.1rem; }
  .ev__item { border-left: 2px solid var(--border-strong); padding-left: 1.1rem; }
  .ev__kind {
    font-size: .7rem; letter-spacing: .08em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-muted); margin: 0 0 .25rem;
  }
  .ev__title { font-size: 1.08rem; margin: 0 0 .4rem; }
  .ev__body { margin: 0 0 .5rem; color: var(--text-soft); font-size: .95rem; line-height: 1.6; }
  .ev__links { margin: 0; display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .9rem; font-size: .86rem; }

  .shot { margin: 1.6rem 0 0; }
  .shot video {
    display: block; width: 100%; height: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 10px; background: var(--bg-sunken);
  }
  .shot figcaption {
    margin-top: .55rem; font-size: .86rem; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--text-muted);
  }
</style>
